A host keeps no place for a CONNECT until the sender shows it is really at its address.

Our own patch (AvP), from our review of ENet 1.3.18 as a reader of hostile packets
(card ENET-E5 in our notes). A CONNECT needs no secret, so anyone could fill every
place with CONNECTs from made-up addresses, and nobody else could join.

- A first CONNECT gets a token back (a new COOKIE command) and nothing is kept. The
  token is SipHash-2-4 of the sender's address, port and connection number and a
  10-second slot, under a key from the system's secure random source, made fresh
  for each slot with the one before still taken. Only a CONNECT that brings the
  token back takes a place, so a token is good for 10 to 20 seconds, from that
  address only. The answer is smaller than the CONNECT.
- The client's ENet puts the token in its CONNECT and sends it again at once.
- A CONNECT from an ENet without the token (8 bytes shorter) is told no at once,
  with a DISCONNECT giving the reason, and the host counts it for the program.
- SipHash is the reference code by Aumasson and Bernstein, copied in as it is by
  portfile.cmake from siphash/ (siphash/README.md says where it came from).

Two copies of this ENet connect as before, with one more round trip.

